## Patterns Coverage KPI — correct denominator (true type population)

Context:
The Coverage KPI read Coverage = covered_count / anchor_count, but the pattern
job sets anchor_count to the *covered* anchor count whenever at least one
pattern exists (it is only the full population when no pattern is found). Since
covered_count = SUM(support) is that same covered set, Coverage was ~always
100% and meaningless. Coverage must be "entities of the type that follow a
pattern / total entities of that type", so the job now persists the full type
population separately and every layer threads it through.

Changes:
1. src/jobs/graph_analytics_job.py
   _run_pattern_stages now computes population_count = anchor_count_query()
   (COUNT of all anchors of the type) unconditionally and adds it to the run
   stats. write_pattern_summary writes a new population_count column into
   <out>_summary.
2. src/back/core/graph_analysis/pattern/JobPatterns.py
   summary() exposes population_count (defaults to 0 when the column is absent
   on older summary tables, so Coverage shows "—" rather than a false 100%).
3. src/back/core/graph_analysis/pattern/saved_pattern_sql.py
   Added population_count to _SAVED_COLUMNS and to both INSERTs (catalog SELECT
   and bulk rest INSERT, via a new population_count parameter).
4. src/back/core/graph_analysis/pattern/SavedPatternStore.py
   Added population_count to _KPI_COLUMNS (auto-migrates existing saved tables)
   and the CREATE TABLE DDL. save() reads population_count from the summary and
   threads it into the per-pattern metrics and insert_rest_sql. patterns() now
   selects max(population_count) and returns it in the run-level stats block.
5. src/front/static/intelligence/js/intelligence-patterns.js
   _coveragePct() now divides covered_count by population_count (the true
   universe). _showPendingSaved prefers the snapshot stats only when
   population_count is present.
6. tests/units/core/test_job_patterns.py,
   tests/units/core/test_pattern_job_sql.py,
   tests/units/core/test_saved_pattern_store.py
   Assert population_count end-to-end, incl. the min_support=2 case where
   population_count (3) differs from anchor_count (2 covered).

Note:
This changes the <out>_summary schema produced by the Databricks pattern job,
so the job must be redeployed (make deploy) and a Detect re-run before the new
denominator exists. Runs saved before the change have no population_count →
Coverage stays "—"; re-running Detect fixes it. A browser hard refresh is
needed to pick up the new static asset.

